WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.210
 

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:11.400
The internet contains zettabytes of
--information that's 10 to the 21st bytes

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:17.340
or 1 billion terabytes, and in the years
to come that number is only going to

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.290
grow. Our advancements in data-driven
technologies, like artificial

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:27.360
intelligence, will also require computers
to achieve greater computation speeds

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.300
than they have today. Currently, our data
is stored in electronic devices using

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:40.350
binary code-- a select pattern of ones and
zeros. To retrieve this data applications

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:46.079
are run to extract and process binary
code, and present the information in its

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:52.050
original form. But this technology has
its limitations, especially as we strive

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:56.420
to design smaller devices. To tackle this
problem,

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:01.370
scientists are turning to alternative
mechanisms of data storage, using

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:07.229
biomolecules, such as DNA, and vast
libraries of synthetic chemicals to

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:15.960
develop innovative approaches to data
storage and computation. DNA is a natural

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:21.740
mechanism of data storage;
it contains all the genetic instructions

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.409
required for our bodies to grow and
function.

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:34.140
If DNA can store all our biological data,
can it store our digital information too?

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.100
Turns out, it can.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:44.670
Like binary code, DNA stores our genetic
information through a sequence of base

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:53.550
units, or nucleotides: Adenine, Guanine, Cytosine and Thymine. With four possible

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:59.490
base units, data that normally requires
two numbers of binary can be stored in

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:05.100
one nucleotide. This means informaticians
can encode twice the amount of

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.800
information in DNA than in binary

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:16.280
So far, scientists have been able to
store entire books and music videos in

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:22.370
DNA sequences. These digital and audio
files are converted into binary code,

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:28.130
that is translated into a specific
sequences of nucleotides-- a strand of DNA

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:34.310
which is then freeze-dried. Once
freeze-dried, this DNA, and the

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:40.100
information it contains, can last for
thousands of years. When the time comes

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:45.230
to access the stored data, the DNA
strands are sequenced and converted back

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:50.410
into binary--or any other base
representation.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:55.820
Besides DNA storage, scientists are
developing new molecular storage

00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:01.970
strategies using a wide variety of small
molecules, including the small molecules

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:08.470
that make up the metabolome. The metabolome 
is the complete set of all small

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:14.750
molecules in a biological system;
it's diverse chemical composition makes

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:20.480
it incredibly information--dense. Think of
all the complex processes that occur in

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:26.299
your body at the molecular level--the
make-up of your metabolome is the informational

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:30.730
basis for all of these events!

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:36.200
Based on this idea, scientists are
creating extensive libraries of

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:41.000
synthetic molecules that, like the
metabolome,  can be used to encode

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:47.930
information, store it, and even execute
complex data computations.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:53.420
With a mixture of these molecules
dissolved in solution, scientists can

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:59.060
retrieve information by analyzing the
mixture with a special instrument. The

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.739
readout from the instrument indicates
the presence or absence of specific

00:04:03.739 --> 00:04:08.870
molecules. This information can be
converted into a base representation

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:14.590
system code, like binary, which a computer
can interpret.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:21.100
For example, the presence of Molecule A
in a sample can be equated to a "1" while

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:26.970
its absence is the equivalent of a "0" in
binary-- referencing a pre-established

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:33.610
library, the presence or absence of a
molecule in a mixture represents one bit

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:41.620
of information. A 4x4 well-plate with 16
unique mixtures of 4 molecules can then

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:48.639
encode 64 bits of data. Imagine this
process on a much larger scale and you

00:04:48.639 --> 00:04:54.910
can get an idea of just how much data
could be stored this way. Additionally, by

00:04:54.910 --> 00:05:00.099
understanding how these molecules will
react in certain environments, scientists

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:05.110
can apply different reaction conditions
to change the data-storing molecules in

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:11.910
a manner akin to traditional computation. In this way, complex manipulations and

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:17.830
calculations can be performed on data in
a multi-dimensional space while it is

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:22.780
stored in a very small volume of
solution. This would allow for

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:27.849
sophisticated parallel computing,
enhanced pattern recognition, and

00:05:27.849 --> 00:05:33.389
improved search algorithms used by
popular web services today.

00:05:33.389 --> 00:05:38.800
Once we were able to harness the storage
and computing powers of small molecules

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:44.889
like DNA and metabolites, we'll be able
to contain more information in less

00:05:44.889 --> 00:05:51.750
volume, and our electronic devices will
become even smaller than they are today.

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:00.040
 

